Cognate with {{cog|sco|tide}}, {{m|sco|tyde||moment, time, occasion, period, tide}}, {{cog|frr|tid||time}}, {{cog|fy|tiid||time, while}}, {{cog|nl|tijd||time}}, {{cog|nl|tij}}, {{m|nl|getij||tide of the sea}}, {{cog|af|tyd||time}}, {{cog|nds|Tied}}, {{m|nds|Tiet||time}}, {{cog|nds|Tide||tide of the sea}}, {{cog|de|Zeit||time}}, {{cog|da|tid||time}}, {{cog|sv|tid||time}}, {{cog|is|tíð||time}}, {{cog|sq|ditë||day}}, {{cog|xcl|տի||age}}, {{cog|kmr|dem||time}}.  {{rel-bottom}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}
{{wikipedia}}

# The [[periodic]] change of the [[sea level]], particularly when caused by the [[gravitational]] influence of the sun and the moon.
# A stream, current or [[flood]].
#: {{synonyms|en|inflood|inflooding|inflow|inflowing|influx}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Timon of Athens|act=III|scene=iv|page=88|column=1|passage=Go I charge thee, inuite them all, let in the '''tide''' / Of Knaues once more: my Cook and Ile provide.}}
# {{lb|en|chronology|obsolete|except in [[liturgy]]}} Time, notably [[anniversary]], period or [[season]] linked to an ecclesiastical feast.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|year=1590|book=1|canto=II|stanza=xxix|passage=[...] and rest their weary limbs a '''tide'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1596|author=w:Edmund Spenser|title=s:Prothalamion
|passage=Which, at th'appointed '''tyde''', / Each one did make his Bryde}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1655|author=w:Thomas Fuller|title=The Church-history of Britain from the Birth of Jesus Christ until the year MDCXLVIII|url=http://www.worldcat.org/title/church-history-of-britain-from-the-birth-of-jesus-christ-until-the-year-m-dc-xlviii/oclc/2602703|text=at the '''tide''' / Of Christ his birth}}
#* {{RQ:Carlyle French Revolution|text=What is singular too, the spademen seem to work lazily; they will not work double-'''tides''', even for offer of more wages, though their '''tide''' is but seven hours[.]}}
# {{lb|en|regional|archaic}} A [[time]].
#: {{ux|en|The doctor's no good this '''tide'''.}}
# {{lb|en|regional|archaic}} A point or period of time identified or described by a qualifier (found in compounds).
#: {{ux|en|Even'''tide''', noon'''tide''', morrow'''tide''', night'''tide''', moon-'''tide''', harvest-'''tide''', winter'''tide''', summer'''tide''', spring'''tide''', autumn-'''tide''' etc.,.}}
# {{lb|en|mining}} The period of twelve hours.
# Something which [[changes]] like the tides of the [[sea]].
# Tendency or direction of causes, influences, or events; course; current.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|act=IV|scene=iii|passage=There is a '''tide''' in the affairs of men, / Which, taken at the flood, leads on to fortune [...]}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Violent [[confluence]]
#* {{RQ:Bacon Henry 7|passage=strong '''Tide'''}}

